<score-partwise version="3.1">
  <!--
    Regression sample for the marks other than staccatos and tenutos at the start or end of a slur, and for fingerings there.
    Gould, Behind Bars, pp. 121-122: only staccato and tenuto marks go inside the first and last notes of a slur; accents at
    the beginning and end of a slur go outside it, so that the slur can remain closer to the note heads, unless they would
    then be too far from the note; pp. 188-189: a pause goes outside a slur that ends at its note. The slur went over them.
    Measure 1: C5 to F5 (stems down), slurred above, an accent on the C5: the accent goes above the slur's start.
    Measure 2: F5 to C5, an accent on the C5, where the slur ends: the accent goes above the slur's end.
    Measure 3: a staccato and an accent on the C5 at the start: the staccato goes inside the slur, the accent outside.
    Measure 4: four F4 eighths (stems up), slurred below, staccatos, and an accent on the first one: the accent goes below
    the slur's start (Gould's example), the staccatos inside.
    Measure 5: G5 down to D5, a fermata on the D5, where the slur ends: the fermata goes above the slur's end, which came
    down onto it.
    Measure 6: a short slur from a C5 with an accent rising steeply to a C6, stems down (Gould's example is from G4 to G5,
    where the slur starts above the middle of the staff anyway): the accent would be too far from the C5 above the slur, so
    it stays inside, and the slur goes over it.
    Measure 7: as measure 1, with a fingering above the accent: the fingering goes above the slur with the accent.
    Measure 8: four chords E5 and G5 with staccatos, slurred, fingerings 1 and 2 on the first one (isc's comment on PR 1828:
    Beethoven's Pathetique, 3rd movement, m. 154): the slur starts beside the fingerings, right of them, past the staccato,
    not over them, from where it would only fall.
    Measure 9: D5 with a tenuto and fingering 4, slurred to the next note (Diabelli, sonatina op. 168 no. 1, m. 1): the slur
    starts beside the fingering.
    Measure 10: G5, F5, E5 and F5, stems up, slurred below the staff, an accent under the G5: the accent is between the note
    and the slur, which passes beyond it, so it stays there.
    Measure 11: G4, C5 and G5, slurred, fingerings 2 and 5 on the C5 and the G5: the slur passes over the 2, so it ends past
    the 5 too, not beside it, which made it rise over the 2 and fall again (Beethoven's Moonlight Sonata, 3rd movement, m. 42,
    see test_slurs_long_steep_arc_moonlight_sonata_issue1466).
  -->
